# How a function takes its arguments.
# Origin: REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3, section 9.
#
# Written before soft-literal parameters exist. They are declared in
# spec/values.allium and gathered by nothing, and the open question there
# asks exactly what the three opt-out forms do.

# --- Normal: evaluated ---------------------------------------

--- id parameters/a-normal-argument-is-evaluated
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 9
--- requires control
--- code
show: func [value] [value]
n: 42
show n
--- result
42

# --- Literal: never evaluated --------------------------------

--- id parameters/a-literal-argument-arrives-as-written
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 9
--- requires control
--- note A quoted word in the spec takes the word itself, which is how ZAP
--- note in the guide can set a variable its caller named.
--- code
name-of: func ['word] [word]
name-of hello
--- result
hello

--- id parameters/a-literal-argument-is-not-looked-up
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 9
--- requires control
--- note Even when the word does name something, the function gets the word.
--- code
defined: 99
name-of: func ['word] [word]
name-of defined
--- result
defined

--- id parameters/a-literal-argument-lets-a-function-assign-for-its-caller
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 9
--- requires control
--- note The guide's ZAP. Taking the word rather than its value is the only
--- note way a function can set something in the caller.
--- code
zap: func ['var] [set var 0]
test: 10
zap test
test
--- result
0

# --- Soft literal: unevaluated unless the caller says otherwise

--- id parameters/a-soft-literal-argument-arrives-as-written
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 9
--- requires control
--- note An ordinary word arrives as written under either sigil, so a
--- note function name arrives without being called. This entry passed
--- note while naming the wrong sigil, because it is the one case where
--- note both behave alike.
--- code
soft: func ['value] [value]
soft hello
--- result
hello

--- id parameters/a-paren-opts-into-evaluation
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 9, corrected against R3 3.22.1
--- requires control
--- note This is the whole point of soft rather than hard. The caller wraps
--- note the argument in a paren to say "evaluate this one after all".
--- note The soft one is written 'value. It was written :value here until a
--- note real R3 was asked, which had the two sigils the wrong way round.
--- code
soft: func ['value] [value]
soft (add 1 2)
--- result
3

--- id parameters/a-get-word-opts-into-evaluation
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 9, corrected against R3 3.22.1
--- requires control
--- code
answer: 42
soft: func ['value] [value]
soft :answer
--- result
42

--- id parameters/a-hard-quoted-paren-is-not-evaluated
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires control
--- note The other sigil, and the contrast that names them. A :value
--- note parameter takes the paren itself, so nothing opts in.
--- code
hard: func [:value] [value]
hard (add 1 2)
--- result
(add 1 2)

--- id parameters/a-hard-quoted-get-word-is-not-evaluated
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires control
--- code
answer: 42
hard: func [:value] [value]
hard :answer
--- result
:answer

--- id parameters/a-soft-literal-still-refuses-an-ordinary-word
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 9
--- requires control
--- note The contrast that makes the two above mean something: without one
--- note of the opt-in forms, the word is not looked up.
--- code
answer: 42
soft: func ['value] [value]
soft answer
--- result
answer

# --- Refinements ---------------------------------------------

--- id parameters/a-refinement-not-asked-for-is-none
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 9
--- requires control
--- note A refinement the caller did not use is NONE rather than unset, so
--- note the body can test it without first asking whether it exists.
--- code
sum: func [a b /average] [
    either average [divide add a b 2] [add a b]
]
sum 123 321
--- result
444

--- id parameters/a-refinement-asked-for-is-true
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 9
--- requires control
--- code
sum: func [a b /average] [
    either average [divide add a b 2] [add a b]
]
sum/average 123 321
--- result
222

--- id parameters/an-unasked-refinements-argument-is-nothing
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The argument belongs to the refinement, so leaving the
--- note refinement off leaves the argument out too. It is none rather
--- note than missing, so the body can read it without asking first.
--- code
f: func [a /opt b] [reduce [a b]]
mold f 1
--- result
"[1 _]"

--- id parameters/asking-for-it-supplies-it
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
f: func [a /opt b] [reduce [a b]]
mold f/opt 1 2
--- result
"[1 2]"

--- id parameters/a-refinement-with-no-argument-of-its-own
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Two refinements where only the second takes an argument, so
--- note the count depends on which were asked for.
--- code
g: func [a /x /y c] [reduce [a c]]
mold g 1
--- result
"[1 _]"
